  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1055 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nDense fog into early Friday morning...Lingering stratus from late\nafternoon has begun to build down and expand as dense fog with \ncooling of the evening. Expect dense fog overnight into early \nFriday morning, expanding farther to the west, before dissipating\nlate in the morning. Dense Fog Advisory issued for most of northeast\nand east-central WI, including the Fox Valley. Only expecting patchy\ndense fog for central and north-central WI, so left those areas out\nof the advisory for now. Repeat performance of dense fog does not\nappear likely on Friday night as southerly flow begins to increase\njust off the surface ahead of cold front tied to strong low crossing\nManitoba Canada. Lakeshore could still see patchy dense fog though.\n\nRain chances...Nothing to write home about, but there are a few\ntimes at least isolated light rain may occur in parts of the area.\nNo widespread rain chances are in store though as strongest lift \nwill remain tied into stronger low pressure systems tracking \nacross Canada. Even so will be a few shortwave troughs/weak fronts\nshifting across the western Great Lakes at times, thus not a completely\ndry forecast. Most favored chances still in line for Saturday night\ninto Sunday morning over the north (20-30%), then Monday night into\nTuesday for especially central WI (20-40%). Beyond this, the weak\nfronts crossing the region will keep small chances of rain through\nlate week with stronger cold front resulting in greater chances of\nrain late Wednesday into Thursday. Even with the more frequent\nchances rain seen compared to the relatively dry pattern of recent,\noverall rainfall amounts look light, under even a half inch for the\nentire time frame.\n\nNext week temperatures...Cooling on the way, most notable Monday \nand Tuesday as highs reach the 50s for most, but may stay in the \n40s far north. A bounce back occurs with highs in the 60s again \nahead of the approaching front for Wednesday and Thursday. May \nturn cooler again late next week as we head toward the end of the \nmonth, but spread from 25th to 75th percentile on NBM is pretty \nhigh, so there are still uncertainties on temps by that time. \nNighttime lows will be seasonable. Monday night lows do drop into\nthe upper 20s to low 30s across the north, though little impacts \nare expected as the growing season has come to an end.",
